Issue #62: Compile-time annotation processor for jjq-mapper (jjq-mapper-processor)

## Overview

Add an optional annotation processor module (`jjq-mapper-processor`) that generates optimized mapping classes at compile time, eliminating runtime reflection, MethodHandle dispatch, and TypeConverter cascades from the data binding hot path of the reflection-based `jjq-mapper` (0.1.11).

## Motivation

Current reflection-based performance on pre-parsed JqValue input vs Jackson:

| Scenario | jjq-mapper (reflection) | Jackson |
|---|---|---|
| Simple record (5 fields), pre-parsed | 115 ns | 129 ns |
| Nested record, pre-parsed | 195 ns | 196 ns |
| List of records, pre-parsed | 423 ns | 378 ns |

A compile-time processor could reduce the pre-parsed path to ~40-50 ns by eliminating:
- `Class.getRecordComponents()` reflection (~eliminated at compile time)
- `MethodHandle.invokeWithArguments(Object[])` for constructor (~20 ns)
- `Object[]` allocation for constructor args (~15 ns)
- `TypeConverter.toJava()` instanceof cascade per field (~25 ns total)
- `FieldMapping` / `ClassMapping` indirection (~10 ns)

The irreducible minimum is the jq program execution: 3 ns per simple field access.

## Use Cases

- Record deserialization/serialization with `@JqField(".path")` custom paths and `@JqIgnore` exclusions
- Zero-configuration fallback: when the generated class is not on the classpath, the existing reflection path (`ClassMapping.forRecord`) is used

## Design

### Module structure

```
jjq-mapper/              — runtime API (unchanged, already exists)
  JqMapper.java           — public API, detects generated mappings
  JqField.java            — @JqField annotation
  JqIgnore.java           — @JqIgnore annotation
  TypeConverter.java      — fallback type conversion
  ClassMapping.java       — reflection-based mapping (fallback)
  GeneratedMapping.java   — NEW: abstract base for generated mappers

jjq-mapper-processor/    — NEW: annotation processor module
  JqMapperProcessor.java  — javax.annotation.processing.Processor impl
  MappingGenerator.java   — JavaPoet or raw String code generation
```

### Generated code example

For:
```java
record User(String name, int age, @JqField(".address.city") String city) {}
```

The processor generates:
```java
package com.example;

import io.hyperfoil.tools.jjq.JqProgram;
import io.hyperfoil.tools.jjq.mapper.GeneratedMapping;
import io.hyperfoil.tools.jjq.mapper.JqMapper;
import io.hyperfoil.tools.jjq.value.*;

public final class User_JqMapping extends GeneratedMapping<User> {
    private static final JqProgram P_NAME = JqProgram.compile(".name");
    private static final JqProgram P_AGE = JqProgram.compile(".age");
    private static final JqProgram P_CITY = JqProgram.compile(".address.city");

    @Override
    public User fromJqValue(JqValue input, JqMapper mapper) {
        return new User(
            P_NAME.apply(input).asString(null),
            (int) P_AGE.apply(input).asLong(0),
            P_CITY.apply(input).asString(null)
        );
    }

    @Override
    public JqValue toJqValue(User instance, JqMapper mapper) {
        return JqObject.builder(3)
            .put("name", instance.name())
            .put("age", (long) instance.age())
            .put("city", instance.city())
            .build();
    }

    @Override
    public Class<User> type() { return User.class; }
}
```

Key properties of generated code:
- **Static JqProgram fields**: compiled once in class initializer, shared across threads
- **Direct constructor call**: `new User(a, b, c)` instead of `MethodHandle.invokeWithArguments(Object[])`
- **Direct accessor calls**: `instance.name()` instead of `MethodHandle.invoke(instance)`
- **Inlined type conversion**: `.asString(null)` instead of `TypeConverter.toJava(value, String.class, genericType, mapper)`
- **No intermediate objects**: no FieldMapping, no Object[], no Optional from tryGet()

### Discovery mechanism

`JqMapper` detects generated mappings via naming convention (`<TypeName>_JqMapping`), with `GeneratedMapping` checked first in the cache, falling back to `ClassMapping.forRecord(t)` reflection:

```java
// In JqMapper.getMapping():
private <T> ClassMapping<T> getMapping(Class<T> type) {
    return cache.computeIfAbsent(type, t -> {
        // Try generated mapping first
        GeneratedMapping<T> generated = loadGenerated(t);
        if (generated != null) return generated;
        // Fall back to reflection
        return ClassMapping.forRecord(t);
    });
}

private <T> GeneratedMapping<T> loadGenerated(Class<T> type) {
    try {
        String generatedName = type.getName() + "_JqMapping";
        Class<?> cls = Class.forName(generatedName);
        return (GeneratedMapping<T>) cls.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
    } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
        return null; // no generated mapping, use reflection
    }
}
```

Simple, zero-configuration: if the generated class is on the classpath it is used; otherwise the reflection path is used. No service loader, no registration — just naming convention.

